NAME
       chmod - change the permission bits of files

SYNOPSIS
       chmod [OPTION...] MODE[,MODE...] FILE...
       chmod [OPTION...] OCTAL-MODE FILE...
       chmod [OPTION...] --reference=RFILE FILE...

DESCRIPTION
       chmod sets who may read, write and run each FILE. Only the file's owner
       and root may change its mode.

       Every file has three sets of permissions: for the owner (u), the group
       (g) and everybody else (o). Each set has read (r), write (w) and
       execute (x). On a directory, r lets you list the names, w lets you
       create, delete and rename entries, and x lets you enter the directory
       and reach the files in it.

   Symbolic modes
       A symbolic mode is WHO, an operator and PERMISSIONS:

       WHO          u (owner), g (group), o (others), a (all three).
                    Without WHO, a is used, minus the bits the umask hides.
       OPERATOR     + adds, - removes, = sets exactly (clears the rest).
       PERMISSIONS  r, w, x; X (execute only for directories, or for files
                    that already have an execute bit); s (set-user-ID with u,
                    set-group-ID with g); t (sticky).

       Join several changes with commas: u+rwx,g=rx,o=.

   Octal modes
       An octal mode adds up r=4, w=2, x=1 for each set: 750 is rwxr-x---.
       A fourth digit in front sets the special bits: 4 set-user-ID,
       2 set-group-ID, 1 sticky. 2770 is rwxrws---.

   Special bits
       set-user-ID (u+s, 4000)
              A program runs with its owner's rights (passwd uses this).

       set-group-ID (g+s, 2000)
              On a directory, new files and subdirectories get the
              directory's group instead of the creator's primary group. This
              is how shared team directories work.

       sticky (o+t, 1000)
              On a directory, only a file's owner (or the directory's owner,
              or root) may delete or rename it, as in /tmp.

       ls -l shows these bits as s or t in the execute position; a capital S
       or T means the bit is set but the execute bit under it is not.

OPTIONS
       -R, --recursive
              Change the files and directories inside each directory too.

       -v, --verbose
              Report every file processed.

       -c, --changes
              Report only the files that really changed.

       -f, --silent, --quiet
              Do not print most error messages.

       --reference=RFILE
              Copy the mode of RFILE instead of giving a MODE.

EXAMPLES
       Let the owner run a script:

           chmod u+x /root/backup.sh

       Owner full access, group read and enter, nothing for others:

           chmod 750 /srv/reports

       A shared directory for the group devs: members can create files, new
       files belong to devs, and nobody else can look inside:

           chgrp devs /shared/devs
           chmod 2770 /shared/devs

       The same with symbolic modes:

           chmod u=rwx,g=rwxs,o= /shared/devs

       Let only owners delete their own files in a public drop folder:

           chmod 1777 /srv/dropbox

       Give everyone read access to a tree, adding x only on directories:

           chmod -R a+rX /var/www/html

NOTES
       chmod never changes symbolic links themselves; it changes the file the
       link points to. Permissions are only part of the answer: ACLs (see
       getfacl(1)) and SELinux can also allow or refuse access.

SEE ALSO
       chown(1), chgrp(1), ls(1), umask (in bash(1)), getfacl(1), setfacl(1)
